1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:06,346
A fuel log with a million and a half rows will not open in a spreadsheet.

2
00:00:06,346 --> 00:00:08,768
Data Noodler is for that job.

3
00:00:08,768 --> 00:00:20,460
It reads big flat files on a locked-down PC, answers questions in plain SQL, and turns the answer into a dashboard, all on your own machine.

4
00:00:20,460 --> 00:00:22,799
The data today is synthetic.

5
00:00:23,049 --> 00:00:25,611
Here is how it fits together.

6
00:00:25,611 --> 00:00:28,619
You double-click Data_Noodler.

7
00:00:28,619 --> 00:00:28,876
bat.

8
00:00:28,876 --> 00:00:36,826
That starts a small PowerShell server that listens on localhost only, and your browser shows the pages.

9
00:00:37,225 --> 00:00:43,016
Behind it is the data engine, written in C and compiled in memory at start.

10
00:00:43,016 --> 00:00:51,747
Tables sit in memory as Arrow, queries spill to disk when memory runs short, and each project is a folder on disk.

11
00:00:52,447 --> 00:00:59,069
Raw files go in: CSV, Parquet, Arrow or SQLite, copied into the project or read in place.

12
00:00:59,769 --> 00:01:03,435
The work in the middle is plain SQL, saved with the project.

13
00:01:04,177 --> 00:01:06,160
Each query builds a clean table.

14
00:01:06,860 --> 00:01:16,268
Out come dashboards as an image, PDF, PowerPoint slide or a one-file web page, and any table as CSV, Parquet or SQLite.

15
00:01:16,968 --> 00:01:19,708
Semaphore can take that CSV as a plain file.

16
00:01:19,708 --> 00:01:26,204
Fairlead and Lazarette are not used: Noodler makes no model calls and holds no keys.

17
00:01:26,904 --> 00:01:39,175
And the guardrails: localhost only with a session token, file access confined to the project, trash and snapshot archives, and a label on every project.

18
00:01:39,175 --> 00:01:42,010
Starting it needs no install and no admin rights.

19
00:01:42,710 --> 00:01:45,637
The control panel shows the web server and the data engine both online.

20
00:01:46,337 --> 00:01:51,104
Every request is listed as it happens, and Stop Server is one click.

21
00:01:51,104 --> 00:01:54,825
If every window is closed, it stops itself after two minutes.

22
00:01:54,825 --> 00:01:59,403
Make a project: a name, a description, and a classification label.

23
00:01:59,403 --> 00:02:04,223
This one is Internal, and the label travels with everything the project publishes.

24
00:02:04,923 --> 00:02:05,746
Import opens next.

25
00:02:05,746 --> 00:02:08,130
Point it at the CSV on a local drive.

26
00:02:08,130 --> 00:02:09,273
A network path works too.

27
00:02:10,172 --> 00:02:14,873
One and a half million rows, a hundred and fourteen megabytes, counted in under a second.

28
00:02:14,873 --> 00:02:19,498
The columns and their types are detected, and you can change them.

29
00:02:20,198 --> 00:02:28,072
Import writes it as an Arrow dataset in a couple of seconds, then checks the row count and the column names against the source before it says done.

30
00:02:28,783 --> 00:02:30,339
Transform is a SQL editor.

31
00:02:30,339 --> 00:02:36,309
The question: fuel per nautical mile, by vessel class and month.

32
00:02:37,009 --> 00:02:44,550
The first try fails, and the error says why: this dialect has no DATE_TRUNC, use TRUNC with MM instead.

33
00:02:44,550 --> 00:02:46,450
So we make that change.

34
00:02:47,150 --> 00:02:47,636
Run again.

35
00:02:47,636 --> 00:02:53,561
One and a half million rows grouped in under two seconds, and a clean table of eighty-eight rows is saved next to the SQL.

36
00:02:53,811 --> 00:02:54,768
Dashboard Studio.

37
00:02:54,768 --> 00:02:58,786
Add a line chart, open its settings, and click Data.

38
00:02:59,615 --> 00:03:05,831
You build the chart by dragging fields: month onto the X axis, litres per nautical mile onto Y.

39
00:03:06,218 --> 00:03:09,291
Then class, to split the lines.

40
00:03:09,291 --> 00:03:12,782
The chart reads the clean table through the engine.

41
00:03:13,406 --> 00:03:16,526
Every class burns more per mile from December to February.

42
00:03:16,526 --> 00:03:21,551
That winter bump is baked into the synthetic data, and the chart finds it.

43
00:03:21,551 --> 00:03:24,511
Publish writes the dashboard as a web page and a PDF.

44
00:03:24,511 --> 00:03:27,563
Image and PowerPoint slide are one tick away.

45
00:03:28,263 --> 00:03:30,803
Any table can be exported too.

46
00:03:30,803 --> 00:03:36,277
Here the clean table goes out as CSV, ready to hand to Semaphore as a plain file.

47
00:03:36,977 --> 00:03:42,918
And the web page opens straight from the file, with no server and no data behind it.

48
00:03:42,918 --> 00:03:44,636
Its header carries the Internal label.

49
00:03:45,386 --> 00:03:50,425
Next month's log arrives, and nobody wants to repeat all of this.

50
00:03:50,425 --> 00:03:54,816
Update re-runs the project's saved setup, in order.

51
00:03:55,471 --> 00:04:02,018
The first run sets the baseline: import, transform, dashboard, publish, and a zipped snapshot, in about twenty seconds.

52
00:04:02,718 --> 00:04:05,324
Now fifty thousand new rows land in the source file.

53
00:04:05,874 --> 00:04:09,699
Update compares the original file's size and date with what it last read.

54
00:04:10,399 --> 00:04:18,624
It sees the change, reads the file again, rebuilds the table, and republishes, in about eleven seconds.

55
00:04:18,624 --> 00:04:21,723
Ninety-two rows now, up from eighty-eight.

56
00:04:21,973 --> 00:04:24,075
Here is the evidence trail.

57
00:04:24,075 --> 00:04:30,550
The project log lists every step with a time: the import, the update, each file published.

58
00:04:31,319 --> 00:04:33,357
Snapshots are zipped copies you can restore.

59
00:04:34,045 --> 00:04:38,096
And in this whole recording the browser made no request to any host but this computer.

60
00:04:38,654 --> 00:04:46,937
Data Noodler does one job: it makes a file too big for a spreadsheet answerable, on your own computer.

61
00:04:46,937 --> 00:04:50,023
It proposes nothing and sends nothing.

62
00:04:50,023 --> 00:04:55,383
The data was synthetic, and the timings are from this one machine.

63
00:04:55,383 --> 00:04:59,362
The SQL, the log and the snapshots are the trail.
